# REAPER: model, workflow, routing

> **In Zed.** Each docs corpus is an MCP server; its tools are prefixed with the server
> name: `reaper_lua_docs_search|get|verify`, `reaper_jsfx_docs_search|get|verify`,
> `reaper_cpp_docs_search|get|verify`, `reaper_actions_search|get|verify`,
> `reaper_rpp_docs_search|get|verify` (state chunk / RPP format).
> Pass `verify` an **absolute** `path`. Load other skills with the `skill` tool.

## 1. Route

This skill is always loaded for REAPER. **Before you read or edit any code, call the
`skill` tool with the language skill** — it holds the traps of that language, and this
skill does not.

**Editing before it is loaded is a mistake, not a shortcut.** Loading it afterwards to
check your work does not undo an edit written without those traps in mind, and they are
exactly what a plausible-looking edit gets wrong.

| the code is | load |
|---|---|
| a ReaScript in Lua (`reaper.*`, ReaImGui) | `reaper-lua` |
| a JSFX effect (EEL2) | `reaper-jsfx` |
| a native extension in C++ | `reaper-cpp` |

Also load `reaper-rpp` when the code reads or writes a state chunk (`Get/Set*StateChunk`)
or the question is how a project/track/item is structured in the RPP format.

Only when you are about to write `ImGui_*` calls (a UI) add `reaper-lua-imgui` or, in C++,
`reaper-cpp-imgui` (also for SWS `BR_*`/`SNM_*` from C++). Scripts without a UI skip them.
A Lua script driving a JSFX is two languages: load both. Python and EEL2 ReaScript are not
served by any corpus: say so rather than translating from another language's signature.

## 2. How to work here

The user writes the code in the editor; you assist. Requests are usually one of: explain an
API or how REAPER does something; write a function or snippet; change part of their file;
find why their script misbehaves; review.

- **A clear request is done in this turn.** Call the tools now — search, read, edit,
  verify — and reply when the work is done. Never describe a tool call and end the turn,
  and never write a tool call as text or in a code block: that executes nothing and the
  user has to type "procedi".
- **Questions get answers, not scripts.** `search` (+ `get` if needed), then answer in a
  few lines with the real signature and, if it helps, a short snippet.
- **Answer the answer.** If you asked a question and the user replies "yes" while adding
  something else, do both: the thing you proposed and the new request.
- **Find every call that touches the same state.** "Keep X unchanged across Y" means every
  call in the code that alters X, not only the one named — e.g. an earlier
  `Main_OnCommand(40652)` (reset rate) changes item length just like the rate loop does.
  Handle them all, or name the ones you left.
- **Their file is theirs.** Read it before changing it. Change only what was asked; keep
  their names, style and structure; no whole-file rewrites and no new files unless asked.
  Something wrong elsewhere? Mention it in one line — do not fix it silently.
- **Match the existing code's patterns** (their `SEP`, their state table, their undo
  wrapper) instead of introducing parallel ones — but check a pattern before you copy it.
  If it is buggy (e.g. `for i = 0, #t` over a table that starts at 0 — it fails when `t`
  is empty), do not replicate it silently: say so in one line.
- **Ask about design, not about execution.** Stop and ask only when you hold more than two
  candidate designs, when you are about to reverse a design you already settled, or when
  the task needs a capability you have not confirmed exists. Then give the options, one
  line each, the trade-off, your recommendation — and stop. Anything else: just do it.
  The corpus settles names and signatures, never architecture: more searching will not
  close a design question.

## 3. The work loop — think briefly, act, check

Every step is **a short thought (a few sentences: what you need next and why) followed by
tool calls**. Long deliberation is the expensive failure here: on a local model it costs
minutes per step, and a tool call answers most doubts faster than reasoning does.

1. **Load** `reaper-scripting` (this) and the language skill, if not loaded.
2. **Read** the code involved, once: the selection, or the function it sits in.
   **Read the card of every `Main_OnCommand(id)` in it — before you edit, never after** —
   with `reaper_actions_get` and
   `Main_<id>` (`"Main_40652"`; a bare number also returns the MIDI-editor actions with the
   same ID) — once per ID per thread, even if a comment names it: its notes
   can carry side effects the name does not mention. If the line has no comment naming
   the action, add one.
3. **Look up only what you will newly write.** Names that stay unchanged in the user's
   code need no lookup. One `search` per operation, described in plain English ("insert a
   new track"), not a half-remembered name — obvious-looking names are routinely fake
   (`GetProject`, `GetItemPosition`, `GetMediaItem_Length`, `CreateTrack`).
   - If the search result shows the full signature, that counts. Call `get` only for the
     card's notes: a field-name list, the meaning of a flag, a pattern (`Begin`/`End`,
     undo, defer).
   - `reaper_actions_get` takes a symbol, never a description: `Main_40519`, or
     `Main__FNG_INCREASERATE` for an extension action. A plain-English name returns
     "Too many matches"; to go from a description to an ID, `reaper_actions_search` first.
   - **Empty search?** The operation may exist only as an action: search
     `reaper_actions`, use `Main_OnCommand`. A direct function beats an action whenever
     one exists.
4. **Edit** the file.
5. **Check what you changed.**
   - You wrote or changed an API call (a `reaper.*` name, a field string, an argument)?
     **verify** the file as saved on disk. Zed's file tools
     return paths relative to the project root; the MCP server cannot resolve those —
     join the root (the folder the project was opened on) and the relative path. Spaces
     are fine.
     DO NOT EXIST → fix that name, verify again. An ERROR is not a pass, and neither is
     NOT CHECKED. If you had to fall back to verifying a list of `names`, **say so** in
     the reply: that run did not check the file.
   - You touched no API name (added `local`, renamed a local, moved a block, changed a
     comment)? `verify` has nothing to say about it: skip it, say so in one line, and run
     the check that fits — `diagnostics` on the file when the task was to clear editor
     warnings.
   - The task was to clear warnings: **`diagnostics` again after the edit**, and report
     what is left.
6. **Reply** (§4).

**Budget.** A typical change is 4–8 tool calls. Past ~8 lookups without an edit, stop
researching: edit with what you have, or ask the one question that unblocks you.
**Do not re-derive what a tool result states** — when a card's notes say how to do
something, do it that way — and do not weigh alternatives the user did not ask about.
**Decide once**: no "let me reconsider" on a settled decision unless a tool result
contradicts it. Unsure between two readings of an API? One more lookup, not a longer
thought. Still ambiguous after that? It is a design question — ask (§2).

**Editor diagnostics are not `verify`.** The Lua language server is fed stubs generated
from this same corpus (`~/.hermes/ai-docs/defs/reaper.lua`: signatures plus each card's
description and notes). They catch wrong arity and wrong types as you type and say nothing
about what a function means, so no diagnostics is not a passed `verify`, and actions and
anything the file does not cover still come from the corpus tools.

`verify` checks the whole file, so it may flag names in code you did not touch: report
those separately ("in your code, line N: `Foo` does not exist"). It is also the first
debugging step when the user's script fails — an invented name is the most common cause.

## 4. What goes in the reply

Short. For an edit the reply has three parts, in this order and nothing else — write the
content, never these labels:

1. one to three lines saying what changed and why;
2. a line starting with `verify:` — `clean`, or the flagged names and what you did, or
   `not run: no API name changed` plus the check you ran instead;
3. one line per thing worth knowing: a pre-existing warning, a pattern you did not fix,
   state the code touches.

No headings, no bold section titles, no restating the reasoning, no code already written
into the file.

- **Always**, after code you wrote or changed: one line with the verify verdict —
  `verify: clean` or the flagged names and what you did about them — plus any NOT CHECKED
  item that concerns your code.
- **If your code touches state it did not create** (render settings, time selection, FX
  chain, files, `gmem`): one line on what is read and saved before, and restored in
  `atexit`.
- **Only for a whole new script, or when asked**: the full verify output, a symbol table
  (names whose signature decided the code, with the `search`/`get` they came from) and a state
  ledger (`state touched | read and saved | restored`, every restore run from `atexit`).

## 5. Rules in every language

- **Confirm the pattern, not just the names.** Undo wrappers, deferred loops, callback
  registration, begin/end pairs differ between APIs; a pattern can be invented while every
  name in it is real. Find it in the docs (`get` notes), not in memory of another tool.
- **Real name, wrong use, is still a bug.** `verify` proves a name exists, never that it is
  used right. A call that does no work the task needs gets deleted.
- **Every write to pre-existing state has a read before it**, and its restore runs from
  `atexit` (a Stop button only fires if pressed).
- **Delete exactly the files you wrote**, by exact path from a list you kept — never a
  pattern or a folder (`<resources>/Effects/` holds every JSFX the user owns).
- **Generated code is code you read.** If one language is emitted as a string of another,
  run the generator on fake data, save the output, read it as source, verify it.
- **One version of each function**, comments that explain the code; your deliberation
  goes in the reply.

## 6. The REAPER object model

The corpora document functions, not what they operate on. A script can use only real names
and still be nonsense: "several items on one track" once became one item with N takes,
verified clean, playing a single sound.

```
ReaProject
 └── MediaTrack            (master track: same type, via GetMasterTrack)
      └── MediaItem
           └── MediaItem_Take
                └── PCM_source
```

Crossing each boundary: `GetTrack(proj, i)`, `GetMasterTrack(proj)`,
`GetMediaItem(proj, i)`, `GetMediaItem_Track(item)`, `GetMediaItemTake(item, i)`,
`GetActiveTake(item)`, `CountTakes(item)`, `GetMediaItemTake_Source(take)`.

- **Project** — `proj = 0` means the active project; it is not an index.
  `EnumProjects(-1)` is the current one, `EnumProjects(i)` enumerates.
- **Track** — the master is a `MediaTrack` too, but outside the `GetTrack` index range.
- **Item** — a block on the timeline: `D_POSITION`, `D_LENGTH`. **A window onto a source,
  not a copy**: which slice shows is the take's `D_STARTOFFS`. Concatenating audio means
  moving windows, not copying samples.
- **Take** — one reading of an item. **Takes are alternatives, not a sequence**: an item
  plays only its active take. N sounds in a row = N items with one take each. A take has
  no position or length; its fields are `D_STARTOFFS`, `D_VOL`, `D_PITCH`, `D_PLAYRATE`.
  A field passed to the wrong family (item vs take vs track) fails silently.
- **Source** — the media, usually a file; `PCM_Source_CreateFromFile(path)`. Several takes
  can share one source.
- **Envelope** — track and take envelopes are the same type (`TrackEnvelope`), only the
  accessor differs (`GetTrackEnvelope` / `GetTakeEnvelope`); the `Envelope*` functions
  serve both. There is no `TakeEnvelope_*` family.
- **FX** — not an object: a (container, index) pair. `TrackFX_*` / `TakeFX_*`.
- **Sends** — not an object: (track, category, index); `GetTrackNumSends(tr, category)`
  counts them, `*TrackSendInfo_Value` reads and writes them by index.
- **Markers and regions** — one enumeration, `EnumProjectMarkers`; `isrgn` tells them
  apart. No separate region API.
- **AudioAccessor** — a temporary reader of samples (`CreateTakeAudioAccessor`); destroy
  it when done.
- **Action sections** (`KbdSectionInfo`, `SectionFromUniqueID`) — an action ID only means
  something together with its section (Main, MIDI Editor, …).

**Handles are stable, indices are not.** `GetTrack`, `GetMediaItem`, `GetMediaItemTake`,
`TrackFX_*` address by position, and every insert, delete or reorder renumbers. So:
collect handles first, then modify (two passes). And an index never outlives the frame
that produced it — in a `defer` script keep a GUID (`TrackFX_GetFXGUID`) or a name, and
resolve the index again where you use it.

**Types from extensions** fail on machines without them: `BR_*`, `CF_Preview`, `FxChain`
(SWS); `PackageEntry` (ReaPack); `JS_*` (js_ReaScriptAPI — a separate extension from
SWS); `ImGui_*` (ReaImGui).
